From 84c28ecc917531702575b03c068a4dded54f5b91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 14:19:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Wide (128-bit) condition zero-tests: fold both halves;
 peephole vector-move extents
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Census-adjacent discovery from the follow-up i128-family sweep (12 shapes x
5 cond sources x 10 boundary values, differential vs GCC): every wide
condition zero-test examined a single slice of the value.

Zero-ness of a wide value is (lo | hi) == 0; a value with a zero low half
and a nonzero high half is NOT zero. Four sites got this wrong:
  - Select slot home: cmpl $0, slot — 4 bytes of a 16-byte slot.
  - Select accumulator home: testq %rax,%rax — low half only.
  - CondBranch fallback: operand_to_rax + testq — low half only.
  - Legacy pushfq paths (x4): same staging.
Probe: sel/selif/neg/usel on (__int128)1 << 64 => FAIL r=201 vs gcc OK.

Fixes, GCC-oracle-shaped:
  - Branch context: emit_wide_cond_branch folds the halves into the
    accumulator (movq slot,%rax; orq slot+8,%rax / orq %rdx,%rax /
    operand_to_rax_rdx + fold) and branches once — the GCC movq/orq/jcc
    shape. A two-cmpq two-jcc form was tried and REJECTED by the sweep:
    a jump after the low-half test is wrong under OR semantics.
  - Select (cmov) context: read-only rcx-form fold (movq lo,%rcx;
    orq hi,%rcx) — flags survive into the cmov; rcx is consumed by arm
    staging strictly after the test; sec cache invalidated per the
    select_operand_to_reg discipline. lccc's select stays branchless
    where GCC branches.
  - Legacy pushfq sites: emit_legacy_cond_test stages the pair and folds
    (pushfq protects the flags across arm staging).
  - Select slot compare sized to the condition's recorded IR type via
    cmp_width_info + new emit_cmp_zero_mem_sized (PR #368's register-
    width rule applied to its stack-slot sibling: reading fewer bytes
    than the store defined is always sound; reading more reads stale
    frame bytes).

The wide test READS the high half of the 16-byte slot — which exposed a
latent tomb: eliminate_dead_stores models packed vector moves as 8-byte
point accesses (cached rbp_offset only), so the i128 parameter's
high-half home store (whose ONLY reader is the movdqu slot copy, extent
[96,112) vs the credited [96,104)) was elided. Pre-existing: masked for
years because the old under-wide test never read that half. The sweep's
loopmut shape flipped to the INVERSE failure once the wide test landed
(loopmut(0) returned the true arm). Fix: vector_frame_move_extent()
(packed SSE/AVX family -> 16/32-byte extent; scalar movsd/movss/SSE-movq
deliberately point-accurate because the FP memory-operand folds depend
on them) + overlap check in eliminate_dead_stores. The blanket
alternative (has_indirect_mem=true for all vector moves) was tried and
REJECTED by test_fp_memory_fold_mulsd (it kills the movsd->mulsd fold);
store_forwarding's 16-byte invalidate is the established range model.

Validation: sweep MATCH vs GCC at O0/O1/O2/O3/Os; units 1679/0/6;
suite PASS=576 FAIL=0 SKIP=15 AB-diff 0.
Tests: tests/regression/wide_cond_zero_test.c (codified sweep) +
check_wide_cond_zero_test.sh (shape contract: branch = movq/orq + single
jcc, select = rcx fold, no cmpl under-read, high-half home store present,
dirty runtime proof) + engineering/AUDIT-2026-09-03-wide-cond-zero-test.md.
